## 1. Original command contract

The driver preserves both requested commands, their package scopes, one execution, and the five-minute Go timeout:

```text
go build ./... && go vet ./... && go test -count=1 -timeout 5m ./...
go test -tags=test_state_path_env -count=1 -timeout 5m ./internal/api/ ./internal/cli/
```

The original Go `-exec` consumer/issuer protocol receives the same image produced for each package by that invocation. Its original four test arguments are retained. The full same-image inventory is required; there is no focused selector or private migration replacement.

Both modes require the original fourteen migration roots, both canonical native roots, and the three API stderr rotation roots. The three CLI panic/error stderr roots remain an additional explicit group. The driver and adapter retain the exact names independently; the configuration must match them. The original required count remains nineteen. Native cleanup subcase evidence is additionally required by the CLI physical owner.

## 2. Executable and Product roles

| Consumer | Actual image | Owner |
|---|---|---|
| CLI full suite | Exact bytes of the incoming Go image, admitted in its own three-file V1 bundle | Separate foreground auxiliary using Source `RunForeground` with required breakaway support |
| API full suite | Exact incoming Go image copied by the namespace owner to its private `r/bin/api.test.exe` | Frozen namespace-v2 auxiliary with original namespace observations |
| Process full suite | Original incoming Go image and all four original test arguments | Existing whole-suite Job entry owner |
| Other packages and inventories | Original incoming image, or the same admitted CLI bytes for its inventory | Existing contained receiver |

The marked Product is built from the complete final TestSource map. Its real publisher epoch and compiler resource describe the CLI incoming image. The CLI receives its version, commit, date, source epoch, GUI subsystem, and the explicit `-s -w` receiver flags through the original Go compiler/linker route. Its binary bytes must remain equal to the incoming image.

A separate tagged `-TestStateIsolatedProduct` has no publisher epoch. Its V1 fixture is supplied as `MCPHUB_CANONICAL_NATIVE_ENTRY_FIXTURE`; tagged API attach-client tests receive this same separate Product through their explicit attach operands. The incoming CLI's `MCPHUB_CANONICAL_ENTRY_FIXTURE` still names the same executing test image. Both Products contain exactly the original three files; no Rust DLL or private3ebe issuer protocol is introduced.

## 3. Physical closure and qualification

The foreground receipt is `literal-foreground-cli-run-v1`. Its receiver binds the exact image digest, original working directory, run root, controller arguments, and the parent issuer's request identity.

Physical closure requires the actual Source native snapshot, empty original `Outcome.Pending`, each acquired native close observation, reader completion and EOF, all original standard-file closes, both capture sinks, and all four path/image pin rows. Every ancestor pin must have its matching original slot close. The fixed source-owned cleanup observations must each have one RUN and one PASS, with no FAIL or SKIP:

1. `TestCanonicalGUIRuntimeRecoveryNativeIndependentReplay`
2. Its `cleanup-uses-current-operation-context` subcase
3. `TestCanonicalSuccessorNativeControlRetainsOriginalFiles`
4. Its `absent` subcase
5. Its `identical` subcase
6. Its `different-identity` subcase
7. `TestStartSupervisorDetachedBreakaway_AddsFlagOnSuccess`

The receipt explicitly describes escaped-actor evidence as a source-pinned test-owned wait/release path. It does not claim a nested close-success receipt or turn snapshots into process authority.

A received original no-child result may close a failed creation only when the actual producer emitted that result and exited nonzero. An early error with no result remains UNKNOWN. Missing cleanup observations, open pins/readers, retained custody, cancellation, or incomplete native observations also remain UNKNOWN.

Normal receiving and the prebound two-argument failed-command callback share one metadata reader and its first error. There is no control-file retry, PID reopen, second wait, new cleanup, or replay in that callback. Failed physical receiving reports closure only and explicitly refuses qualification, capture, cache, and new-command grants. PASS additionally requires the original zero exit, successful full ledger, unchanged input image/bundle, and the existing deadlines.

Namespace receiving uses the already frozen v2 image-copy and seven-API-root fragments. It calls namespace qualification after physical receiving; closure alone cannot qualify a test.

